      "🔹 WHAT IS JSX?\nJSX stands for JavaScript XML. It is a syntax extension for JavaScript that allows developers to write HTML-like markup inside JavaScript files.\n\nExample:\nfunction App() {\n  return <h1>Hello React!</h1>;\n}\n\n🔹 KEY RULES & FEATURES OF JSX\n1. Must return a single root element (or use React Fragments <>...</>).\n2. Attribute names use camelCase (e.g., className instead of class, htmlFor instead of for).\n3. You can embed JavaScript expressions inside curly braces {}.\n4. All tags must be explicitly closed (e.g., <img />, <input />).\n\n🔹 HOW DOES IT WORK?\nJSX isn't understood directly by browsers. Build tools like Vite/Babel compile JSX into standard JavaScript function calls (React.createElement).\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What is JSX?\n👉 A syntax extension for JavaScript that allows writing HTML-like structures within React components.\n\n2️⃣ Can browsers execute JSX directly?\n👉 No. Browsers require a transpiler (like Babel) to convert JSX into standard JavaScript function calls.\n\n3️⃣ Why do we use className instead of class in JSX?\n👉 Because class is a reserved keyword in JavaScript.",
