    description:
      "🔹 WHAT IS A COMPONENT?\nA component is a self-contained, reusable piece of UI in a React application. Applications are built by combining multiple components.\n\nApp ⬇️ Header ⬇️ ProductList ⬇️ ProductCard ⬇️ Button\n\n🔹 REAL-TIME EXAMPLE\nInstead of writing an entire e-commerce app in one large file, we break it into:\n✅ Navbar\n✅ SearchBar\n✅ ProductList\n✅ ProductCard\n✅ Cart & Footer\n\n🔹 FUNCTION COMPONENTS\nModern React uses functional components:\nfunction ProductCard({ name, price }) {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h3>{name}</h3>\n      <p>₹{price}</p>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\n🔹 COMPONENT NAMING & STRUCTURE\n📌 Must start with a Capital Letter (e.g., ProductCard) so React distinguishes user components from built-in HTML tags.\n📌 Organize components into clean files under src/components/Header.jsx.\n\n🔹 COMPONENT TREE\nApp ├── Header ├── Sidebar ├── ProductList (ProductCard, ProductCard) └── Footer\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What is a component in React?\n👉 A reusable, independent unit of UI with its own rendering structure and optional state/behavior.\n\n2️⃣ What are the common types of React components?\n👉 Functional components (modern standard using Hooks) and Class components (legacy).\n\n3️⃣ Why must React component names start with a capital letter?\n👉 Capitalization allows React to differentiate custom components from standard HTML elements.\n\n4️⃣ What is component reusability?\n👉 Writing UI logic once and rendering it in multiple places with different data inputs (props).",
