    image: "public/REACT_POSTS/Day5.png",
    description:
      '🔹 WHAT ARE PROPS?\nProps stands for Properties. Props are used to pass data from a parent component to a child component:\nParent Component ⬇️ Props ⬇️ Child Component\n\n🔹 REAL-TIME EXAMPLE\nImagine an e-commerce application. We have one reusable ProductCard component. Instead of creating separate components for every product, we pass different product information using props:\n\nfunction ProductCard({ name, price }) {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h2>{name}</h2>\n      <p>₹{price}</p>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\n🔹 HOW DO PROPS WORK?\nThe parent passes data:\n<ProductCard name="Laptop" price={75000} />\n\nAnd the child receives it through function parameters.\n\n🔹 SUPPORTED DATA TYPES\nProps can pass: Strings, Numbers, Booleans, Arrays, Objects, and Functions.\n\n🔹 WHY DO WE USE PROPS?\n✅ Component reusability\n✅ Dynamic UI rendering\n✅ Avoiding duplicate code\n✅ Structured parent-child communication\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What are props in React?\n👉 Props are inputs passed from a parent component to a child component.\n\n2️⃣ Can props be modified by the child component?\n👉 No. Props are read-only; child components must treat them as immutable.\n\n3️⃣ Can we pass functions as props?\n👉 Yes. Passing functions as props allows child components to trigger actions in the parent.\n\n4️⃣ What is the difference between props and state?\n👉 Props are external inputs passed to a component, while state is internally managed data.\n\n💡 PRO TIP\nAvoid hardcoding values inside child components. Use props to transform static components into reusable UI blocks.',
