    description:
      '🔹 WHAT IS STATE?\nState is data managed internally by a React component. When state updates, React re-renders the component to reflect changes in the UI:\nState Update ⬇️ React Re-render ⬇️ Updated UI\n\n🔹 useState HOOK\nIn functional components, state is created using the useState Hook:\n\nimport { useState } from "react";\n\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\n🔹 HOW DOES useState WORK?\nuseState returns an array containing two elements: [currentState, setterFunction]. Calling the setter function updates the state value and schedules a UI re-render.\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What is state in React?\n👉 State is internal component data that can change over time and trigger re-renders.\n\n2️⃣ How do you create state in a function component?\n👉 Using the useState Hook.\n\n3️⃣ What happens when state changes?\n👉 React re-renders the component to display the updated state in the UI.\n\n4️⃣ What is the difference between state and props?\n👉 State is internal and mutable by the component, whereas props are read-only parameters passed down from a parent.\n\n💡 PRO TIP\nOnly store values in state if they change over time and directly affect rendering.',
