    description:
      "🔹 WHAT IS EVENT HANDLING?\nEvent handling means responding to user interactions such as clicks, typing, hover events, and form submissions.\n\n🔹 COMMON EVENTS\n🖱️ onClick — Button clicks\n⌨️ onChange — Input field changes\n📝 onSubmit — Form submissions\n👆 onMouseEnter / onMouseLeave — Pointer movements\n⌨️ onKeyDown — Keyboard press events\n\n🔹 PASSING PARAMETERS\nTo pass arguments to an event handler, wrap it in an inline arrow function:\n<button onClick={() => handleDelete(101)}>Delete</button>\n\n🔹 EVENT HANDLING FLOW\nUser Action ⬇️ Synthetic Event ⬇️ Handler Function ⬇️ State/Logic Update ⬇️ UI Re-render\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ How do you handle events in React?\n👉 Using synthetic event handler props like onClick, onChange, and onSubmit.\n\n2️⃣ What is the difference between onClick={handleClick} and onClick={handleClick()}?\n👉 onClick={handleClick} passes a function reference. onClick={handleClick()} executes the function immediately during render.\n\n3️⃣ How do you access an input's current value?\n👉 Access it via the event object: event.target.value.\n\n4️⃣ Why do we use event.preventDefault()?\n👉 To prevent default browser behaviors like full-page refreshes on form submission.\n\n💡 PRO TIP\nKeep event handlers focused and clean. Delegate complex business logic to dedicated helper functions.",
