      "🔹 WHAT IS CONDITIONAL RENDERING?\nConditional rendering lets components display different UI elements or layouts based on underlying conditions or application state.\n\n🔹 COMMON PATTERNS\n1. if...else — Best for large block logic outside JSX.\n2. Ternary Operator (condition ? <TrueUI /> : <FalseUI />) — Ideal for inline conditional rendering.\n3. Logical AND (condition && <UI />) — Used to render UI only when a condition evaluates to true.\n4. Variable Assignment — Storing JSX blocks in variables before returning.\n\n🔹 USE CASES\n📌 Auth flows (login / dashboard)\n📌 Loading spinners & error messages\n📌 Empty list placeholders\n📌 Role-based access control\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What is conditional rendering in React?\n👉 Displaying different UI components based on conditions.\n\n2️⃣ What is the difference between && and the ternary operator?\n👉 && renders UI only when true; the ternary operator handles both true and false outcomes.\n\n3️⃣ Can conditional rendering depend on state?\n👉 Yes. State changes update conditions and trigger re-renders with the appropriate UI branch.\n\n💡 PRO TIP\nAvoid bloated inline JSX logic. Extract complex conditional branches into variables or sub-components.",
