      '🔹 WHAT ARE FORMS IN REACT?\nForms allow users to input and submit data (e.g., logins, signups, search bars, checkout forms).\n\n🔹 CONTROLLED COMPONENTS\nA controlled component is a form input whose value is driven by React state.\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [name, setName] = useState("");\n\n  function handleSubmit(event) {\n    event.preventDefault();\n    console.log("Form submitted:", name);\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\n      <input\n        type="text"\n        value={name}\n        onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)}\n        placeholder="Enter your name"\n      />\n      <button type="submit">Submit</button>\n    </form>\n  );\n}\n\n🔹 BENEFITS OF CONTROLLED FORMS\n✅ Direct access to input values via state\n✅ Instant field validation\n✅ Predictable UI state and easy API integration\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What is a controlled component?\n👉 A form element whose value is tied to state and updated via an onChange handler.\n\n2️⃣ Why do we use onChange?\n👉 To capture input keystrokes and keep state in sync with user input.\n\n3️⃣ Why do we use event.preventDefault()?\n👉 To prevent standard browser form submissions and page reloads.\n\n4️⃣ How do you handle multiple form fields?\n👉 Store inputs in a single state object and update properties dynamically using input name attributes.\n\n💡 PRO TIP\nFor large, complex forms with multiple inputs and validation rules, manage form state as an object or use libraries like React Hook Form.',
