    description:
      "🔹 WHAT IS useState?\nuseState is a React Hook that allows a functional component to store and manage state.\n\nExample:\nimport { useState } from \"react\";\n\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n  return <h2>Count: {count}</h2>;\n}\n\nHere:\ncount → Current state value\nsetCount → State update function\n0 → Initial state\n\n🔹 WHY DO WE NEED useState?\nNormal JavaScript variables don't provide React with a mechanism to trigger a UI update when their values change. With useState, calling the setter function informs React that state has changed, scheduling a re-render to update the component's UI.\n\n🔹 RULES OF useState\n✅ Call Hooks at the top level of your component\n✅ Don't call Hooks inside loops or conditions\n✅ Use the setter function to update state (don't mutate directly)\n✅ Use functional updates (setState(prev => newValue)) when the next value depends on the previous value\n\n🔹 REAL-WORLD USE CASES\n📌 Form inputs, todo lists, counters, search fields, filters, modal visibility, shopping carts, and loading/error states.\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What is useState?\n👉 A React Hook used to add state to functional components.\n\n2️⃣ What does useState return?\n👉 An array containing the current state value and a function to update that state.\n\n3️⃣ Why should we use functional updates?\n👉 Functional updates ensure state updates are based on the latest pending state when next state depends on previous state.\n\n4️⃣ Can useState store objects and arrays?\n👉 Yes, state can store any JavaScript data type.\n\n5️⃣ Can we modify state directly?\n👉 No. State should be treated as immutable; always use the setter function.",
