      "🔹 WHAT IS COMPONENT LIFECYCLE?\nA component lifecycle describes the stages a component goes through during its existence in an application:\n🟢 Mounting → Component created and inserted into DOM\n🟡 Updating → Component re-renders due to state or prop changes\n🔴 Unmounting → Component removed from DOM\n\n🔹 REAL-TIME EXAMPLE: CHAT ROOM\nComponent Mounts ⬇️ Connect to chat server\nRoom Changes ⬇️ Disconnect previous room ⬇️ Connect to new room\nComponent Unmounts ⬇️ Disconnect & Cleanup\n\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const connection = createConnection(roomId);\n  connection.connect();\n  return () => connection.disconnect();\n}, [roomId]);\n\n🔹 WHY IS LIFECYCLE IMPORTANT?\n✅ Helps fetch data at appropriate times\n✅ Manages timers and event listeners safely\n✅ Prevents memory leaks by ensuring proper resource cleanup\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What are the main stages of a React component lifecycle?\n👉 Mounting, Updating, and Unmounting.\n\n2️⃣ How do function components handle lifecycle events?\n👉 Primarily through the useEffect Hook paired with dependency arrays and cleanup functions.\n\n3️⃣ What is the purpose of the cleanup function?\n👉 To release or disconnect external resources created by an effect when a component updates or unmounts.\n\n4️⃣ What are common lifecycle methods in class components?\n👉 componentDidMount(), componentDidUpdate(), and componentWillUnmount().",
