      "🔹 WHAT IS LIFTING STATE UP?\nLifting state up means moving shared state to the closest common parent component so that multiple child components can consume and sync with the same source of truth.\n\nInstead of:\nChild A (Own State) ❌ Child B (Own State) — Hard to keep synchronized.\n\nUse:\nCommon Parent (Shared State) ⬇️ Props ⬇️ Child A & Child B\n\n🔹 LIFTING STATE vs GLOBAL STATE\n📌 Lifting state: Uses standard React props and parent state; best for nearby sibling components.\n📌 Global state: Uses Context API or libraries like Redux; best for widely shared data across distant tree nodes.\n\n🔹 WHY DO WE LIFT STATE?\n✅ Maintains a single source of truth\n✅ Keeps sibling components in sync\n✅ Prevents duplicated state logic\n✅ Makes data flow predictable\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What is lifting state up?\n👉 Moving shared state to the nearest common parent component.\n\n2️⃣ How can a child component update state owned by its parent?\n👉 The parent passes a callback function down via props, which the child invokes with updated values.\n\n3️⃣ What is the benefit of a single source of truth?\n👉 Eliminates duplicate state conflicts and keeps the UI consistent across all components.",
