    description:
      '🔹 WHAT IS A CONTROLLED COMPONENT?\nIn a controlled component, React state controls the input value. The input value is stored in state and updated through an event handler.\n\nExample:\nconst [name, setName] = useState("");\n<input value={name} onChange={(event) => setName(event.target.value)} />\n\nFlow: User Types ⬇️ onChange ⬇️ setName() ⬇️ React State ⬇️ Input Value\n\n🔹 WHAT IS AN UNCONTROLLED COMPONENT?\nAn uncontrolled component maintains its own internal state in the DOM. Instead of writing an event handler for every state update, you use a ref to pull values from the DOM when needed.\n\n🔹 IMPORTANT: DON\'T MIX THEM CARELESSLY\nAn input should not switch unexpectedly between controlled and uncontrolled. Keep the input consistently controlled or uncontrolled for its entire lifetime.\n\n🔹 WHY USE CONTROLLED VS UNCONTROLLED?\n📌 Controlled: Predictable state, instant validation, easy conditional rendering, better control over complex forms.\n📌 Uncontrolled: Simple implementation, less React state, ideal when values are only needed at form submission.\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What is a controlled component?\n👉 A form element whose value is controlled by React state.\n\n2️⃣ What is an uncontrolled component?\n👉 A form element whose value is managed by the DOM and accessed through a ref.\n\n3️⃣ What is the main difference?\n👉 Controlled → React is the source of truth | Uncontrolled → DOM is the source of truth.\n\n💡 PRO TIP\nUse controlled components by default for forms that require validation or dynamic behavior. For basic forms where values are only needed upon submission, uncontrolled components with refs are completely fine.',
