    image: "public/REACT_POSTS/Day18.png",
    description:
      '🔹 WHAT ARE CSS MODULES?\nCSS Modules allow you to write CSS that is locally scoped to a specific component, preventing global style pollution and accidental class name collisions.\n\nFlow: Component ⬇️ CSS Module ⬇️ Scoped Styles ⬇️ No Naming Conflicts\n\n🔹 BASIC CSS MODULE EXAMPLE\nFile: Button.module.css\n.primary {\n  background: blue;\n  color: white;\n}\n\nFile: Button.jsx\nimport styles from "./Button.module.css";\nfunction Button() {\n  return <button className={styles.primary}>Submit</button>;\n}\n\n🔹 BENEFITS OF CSS MODULES\n✅ Locally scoped class names\n✅ Eliminates class naming collisions\n✅ Better component isolation\n✅ Supports native CSS features (media queries, pseudo-classes, keyframes)\n\n🔹 COMMON MISTAKES\n❌ Forgetting the `.module.css` file extension\n❌ Using string class names `className="button"` instead of `{styles.button}`\n❌ Mixing global styles without a clear strategy\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What are CSS Modules?\n👉 A styling approach where CSS class names are scoped locally to individual components.\n\n2️⃣ How do you import and apply a CSS Module?\n👉 `import styles from \'./Component.module.css\';` and apply via `className={styles.className}`.\n\n3️⃣ Do CSS Modules support media queries?\n👉 Yes, they support all standard CSS features including media queries, animations, and pseudo-selectors.',
