      "🔹 WHAT IS DEBUGGING?\nDebugging is the systematic process of finding, understanding, and fixing issues (runtime errors, broken UI renders, incorrect state, or unexpected API responses) in an application.\n\nFlow: Error ⬇️ Reproduce ⬇️ Inspect ⬇️ Identify Root Cause ⬇️ Apply Fix ⬇️ Test\n\n🔹 COMMON DEBUGGING TOOLS\n📌 React Developer Tools (inspect component trees, props, state, hooks)\n📌 Browser DevTools & Console (`console.log`, breakpoints, source maps)\n📌 Network Tab (inspect HTTP status codes: 200, 400, 401, 403, 404, 500)\n\n🔹 TROUBLESHOOTING COMMON REACT ISSUES\n👉 Infinite Loops: Usually caused by missing or incorrect `useEffect` dependency arrays or updating state inside effects without guards.\n👉 Broken List Renders: Check if keys are missing, non-unique, or changing on every render (e.g., using `Math.random()`).\n👉 Blank UI/Failed Renders: Ensure state values are not `undefined` before accessing nested properties.\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What is React Developer Tools?\n👉 A browser extension used to inspect React component hierarchies, props, state, and hooks in real time.\n\n2️⃣ How do you debug API calls in React?\n👉 Use the browser Network tab to inspect request headers, payloads, response status codes, and JSON outputs.\n\n3️⃣ What is an Error Boundary?\n👉 A React class component mechanism used to catch rendering errors anywhere in a child component tree and display a fallback UI.",
