      "🔹 RENDERING LISTS WITH map()\nReact uses JavaScript's `Array.prototype.map()` to transform data arrays into lists of JSX elements.\n\n🔹 THE IMPORTANCE OF KEYS\n📌 Keys provide React with a stable identity across renders to track which list items are added, updated, or removed.\n📌 Always use unique, stable IDs (e.g., `key={task.id}`). Avoid using array indexes when lists can be reordered, filtered, or mutated.\n\n🔹 CONDITIONAL RENDERING PATTERNS\n👉 Ternary Operator: `{isLoggedIn ? <UserDashboard /> : <LoginForm />}`\n👉 Logical AND (&&): `{hasUnreadMessages && <NotificationBadge />}`\n👉 Empty State Guards: Handle empty collections gracefully (`if (items.length === 0) return <EmptyMessage />`).\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ Why are stable keys crucial when rendering lists?\n👉 They enable React's reconciliation algorithm to efficiently re-render only modified items rather than re-creating the entire DOM list.\n\n2️⃣ What happens if you use array index as a key for dynamic lists?\n👉 It causes unexpected UI state bugs, broken input tracking, and performance degradation during reordering or deletion.",
