    description:
      "🔹 WHAT IS useEffect?\n`useEffect` is a React Hook designed to synchronize functional components with external systems, such as browser APIs, network requests, subscriptions, and timers.\n\n🔹 DEPENDENCY ARRAY RULES\n📌 No Array (`useEffect(fn)`): Runs after every single render.\n📌 Empty Array (`useEffect(fn, [])`): Runs once after initial mount.\n📌 Dependencies (`useEffect(fn, [tasks])`): Runs after mount and whenever dependency values change.\n\n🔹 PERSISTING STATE WITH LOCALSTORAGE\n📌 Lazy Initialization: Read from LocalStorage once during state setup: `const [tasks, setTasks] = useState(() => JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('tasks') || '[]'))`.\n📌 Sync via Effect: Save updates whenever state changes: `useEffect(() => { localStorage.setItem('tasks', JSON.stringify(tasks)); }, [tasks])`.\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ Why use lazy initialization in useState for LocalStorage?\n👉 Passing a function ensures LocalStorage (a synchronous I/O operation) is read only once during initial mount rather than on every re-render.\n\n2️⃣ How do JSON.stringify and JSON.parse help with LocalStorage?\n👉 LocalStorage only stores strings; `stringify` converts objects/arrays to strings, and `parse` converts strings back into JavaScript data structures.",
