      "🔹 DYNAMIC SEARCH & FILTERING\nCombine input state (`search`, `filter`) with native `filter()` and `includes()` array operations to produce dynamically filtered collections without extra state duplication.\n\n🔹 CALCULATING DERIVED STATISTICS\nAvoid creating separate state variables for derived counts (e.g., `totalTasks`, `completedTasks`). Derive them directly from primary state during render:\n```jsx\nconst totalTasks = tasks.length;\nconst completedTasks = tasks.filter(t => t.completed).length;\nconst pendingTasks = totalTasks - completedTasks;\n```\n\n🔹 FILTERING IMPLEMENTATION\n```jsx\nconst filteredTasks = tasks.filter(task => {\n  const matchesSearch = task.title.toLowerCase().includes(search.toLowerCase().trim());\n  const matchesFilter = filter === 'completed' ? task.completed : filter === 'pending' ? !task.completed : true;\n  return matchesSearch && matchesFilter;\n});\n```\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ Why should derived values NOT be stored in separate state?\n👉 Storing derived values in state causes redundant re-renders, sync bugs, and unnecessary state management overhead. Calculate them directly in regular render logic.\n\n2️⃣ How does search filtering work in React?\n👉 Maintain a search query string state and derive a filtered sub-array on each render using `Array.prototype.filter()`.",
