    description:
      "🔹 WHAT IS THE CONTEXT API?\nThe Context API provides a way to share global/tree-wide state across a component subtree without manually passing props through intermediate components (solving **Prop Drilling**).\n\n🔹 CORE CONTEXT STEPS\n1️⃣ Create Context: `const ThemeContext = createContext(defaultValue);`\n2️⃣ Provide Value: `<ThemeContext.Provider value={theme}> {children} </ThemeContext.Provider>`\n3️⃣ Consume Value: `const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);` inside any descendant component.\n\n🔹 PROP DRILLING VS CONTEXT API\n📌 Prop Drilling: Explicitly passing props down through multiple layout/wrapper layers (e.g., `App` ➔ `Dashboard` ➔ `Profile` ➔ `UserInfo`).\n📌 Context API: Allows deep descendants (`UserInfo`) to read values directly from the nearest `Provider` up the component tree.\n\n🔹 RE-RENDER CONSIDERATIONS & BEST PRACTICES\n⚠️ When a Context `Provider` value changes, **all** consumer components that call `useContext` will automatically re-render.\n✅ Split unrelated concerns into separate contexts (e.g., `AuthContext`, `ThemeContext`).\n✅ Stabilize complex context objects using `useMemo` to prevent unintended updates.\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What is prop drilling in React?\n👉 The practice of passing props through intermediate components that do not need the data, solely to deliver it to a deeply nested child.\n\n2️⃣ Does updating a Context Provider trigger re-renders in consumer components?\n👉 Yes. All components consuming the context via `useContext` or `Context.Consumer` will re-render when the Provider's `value` changes.",
