    description:
      "🔹 PROPS VS. CONTEXT API\n📌 Props: Used for explicit, predictable parent-to-child data flow. Best for component-specific configurations, UI reusability, and clear dependency tracking.\n📌 Context API: Used to share global or tree-wide data across multiple descendant components without passing props through intermediate layers, effectively solving **prop drilling**.\n\n🔹 WHEN TO CHOOSE WHICH\n✅ Choose Props when: Data flows down a simple parent-child hierarchy and only a few immediate descendants require the values.\n✅ Choose Context API when: Many components across different nesting levels need access to shared global concerns like themes (`ThemeContext`), authentication (`AuthContext`), or language settings.\n\n🔹 PERFORMANCE CONSIDERATIONS\n⚠️ When a Context Provider's value changes, all consumer components calling `useContext` will automatically re-render. Avoid putting all application state into one giant context; instead, split unrelated concerns into separate contexts.\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What is the difference between Props and Context API?\n👉 Props are passed explicitly down the component tree from parent to child; Context API shares values across a subtree globally without requiring intermediate component prop passing.\n\n2️⃣ Does the Context API replace props completely?\n👉 No. Props are still essential for component reuse, customizability, and maintaining clear parent-child interfaces. Context should only be used for genuinely shared tree-wide data.",
