      "🔹 WHAT IS STATE MANAGEMENT?\nState management is the process of storing, updating, and sharing data across a React application (e.g., shopping carts, logged-in user info, dark/light themes, search filters).\n\n🔹 TYPES OF STATE IN REACT\n📌 Local UI State: Component-specific data like modal visibility, input values, and toggles (managed via `useState` or `useReducer`).\n📌 Global State: Shared data needed across multiple components like user preferences or authentication status (managed via Context API or state libraries).\n📌 Server State: Asynchronous data fetched from APIs, including loading states, errors, and caching.\n📌 URL State: Parameters, filters, pagination, and tabs reflected directly in the browser URL.\n\n🔹 STATE MANAGEMENT APPROACHES\n✅ `useState`: Best for local component-specific UI state.\n✅ Props: Best for direct parent-to-child data flow.\n✅ Context API: Best for avoiding prop drilling across a component subtree (themes, auth state).\n✅ External Libraries (Redux Toolkit, Zustand): Best for complex, highly scaled global state requirements.\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What is state management in React?\n👉 The architectural strategy of handling data storage, updates, and accessibility across UI components.\n\n2️⃣ What is the difference between client state and server state?\n👉 Client state is UI-focused data managed locally in memory; server state originates from a remote database/API and requires asynchronous fetching, caching, and synchronization.",
